* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
* show.doc  -  print an X-type record from SDSYS's VOC, a field to a line.
*
* SDSYS's VOC RATHER THAN THIS ACCOUNT'S, so there is one copy on the system
* and it is reachable from wherever CONFIG was typed.  The same @sdsys route
* LOGIN uses for batch.jobs.
*
* PAGINATION IS SD's OWN.  Turning it on for the duration is what replaces the
* pager, and it is put back afterwards whichever way this leaves - a verb that
* silently changed the session's paging would be a worse bug than the one
* being fixed.
* ----------------------------------------------------------------------

show.doc:
*  17 Nov 26 - the shipped file @sdsys/<doc.name>, a line at a time (see show.contrib).  doc.rec starts with 'X' in field 1 so the
*  loop below, written for an X record whose text started at field 2, is unchanged.
   doc.path = @sdsys : @ds : doc.name
*  19 Nov 26 - RELEASE_1.1 353: READONLY, as audit_read opens the same folder.  Without it an ordinary account was told "Cannot open"
*  (owner's host, 10:43 install): the file is read-only to sdusers, and the read-write open failed; this program never writes it.
   openseq doc.path readonly to doc.f else
      display 'Cannot open ' : doc.path : ', so it cannot be shown.'
      return
   end
   doc.rec = 'X'
   loop
      readseq doc.line from doc.f else exit
      doc.rec<-1> = doc.line
   repeat
   close doc.f

   doc.lines = dcount(doc.rec, @fm)
   if doc.lines < 2 then
      display doc.path : ' holds no text.'
      return
   end

*  THE SAME PAGINATION IDIOM CPROC USES (CPROC:525), not SETPU: SETPU takes a
*  numeric PU$ key and a unit number and is for print units, while pagination
*  is a kernel setting.  Read it, turn it on, put it back.
   pagination.state = kernel(K$PAGINATE, -1)
   void kernel(K$PAGINATE, @true)

   crt @(-1) :
*  Field 1 is the record type, X.  The text starts at field 2.
   for doc.i = 2 to doc.lines
      print doc.rec<doc.i>
   next doc.i

   void kernel(K$PAGINATE, pagination.state)
return

